WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
[Submit subtitle corrections at criblate.com]

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.000
Imagine yourself in an art museum.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.503
As you stroll through the galleries, you come across a

00:00:07.503 --> 00:00:10.720
large low table covered in small white skeletal frames.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.520
Each looks like a random fragment of a cube, and they're all laid out in an orderly grid.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.568
What we're looking at is a digital reproduction of an artwork

00:00:19.568 --> 00:00:22.960
called Variations of Incomplete Open Cubes by Sol LeWitt.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.640
It represents every possible way that a cube can be incomplete,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.260
subject to a few conditions.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.347
In this video, you and I will investigate this sculpture

00:00:31.347 --> 00:00:33.860
and explore some of the mathematical ideas behind it.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.961
We'll get to know these cubes, making visualizations,

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:40.740
observing patterns and asking simple questions with surprising answers.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.476
Although I've tried to make these explorations accessible and playful,

00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:47.220
in the end we'll stumble into a highly non trivial result.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.129
A lovely classical technique, an ingenious trick

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:52.820
for counting cubes by thinking about symmetry.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.020
But first, let's get a little more specific about what we're looking at here.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.180
First of all, a cube is of course, a regular six sided shape.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.980
By open cube, we mean a hollow cube framed by its 12 edges.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.180
We can make an open cube incomplete by selecting one of these edges and getting rid of it.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.260
By removing other edges, we can create different variations.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.550
The first of LeWitt's constraints is that the existing edges must all be connected.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:29.630
We can't remove so many edges that the cube falls apart into separate pieces.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:35.550
The second constraint basically says that the existing edges have to form an obvious cube.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.950
If we reduce our cube down to, say, a flat square, we're going to say that doesn't count.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.790
Nor does anything less.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.510
A corner, a single edge, or nothing at all.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.204
Our cubes must be properly three dimensional, meaning they have to

00:01:48.204 --> 00:01:51.580
have at least one edge along the height, width and depth of the cube.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.672
To understand the final constraint, let's bring

00:01:56.672 --> 00:01:59.020
in another open cube and make it incomplete.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.906
This may look different than the first cube, but if we grab it and tumble it

00:02:03.906 --> 00:02:08.460
around like this, we can see that it is actually the same incomplete open cube.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.460
We were just looking at it from a different angle.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.718
In fact, if we played around with this shape long enough,

00:02:13.718 --> 00:02:17.605
we would eventually discover that there are 24 different ways we can reposition it,

00:02:17.605 --> 00:02:21.400
each of which looks unique from the others, but all fundamentally the same shape.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.006
In the final art piece, it would be a little redundant

00:02:25.006 --> 00:02:27.800
to include all of these rotationally equivalent figures.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.600
So we just choose one to represent the whole family.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.520
We then place this representative on the table.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.320
The next cube will be rotationally distinct from this one, a genuinely different shape.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.680
The next cube is rotationally unique from those and so on for the whole table.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.135
The final work of art shows the full collection

00:02:47.135 --> 00:02:50.640
of 122 rotationally unique incomplete open cubes.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.194
One of the interesting things about this piece is that the

00:02:54.194 --> 00:02:57.360
entire definition can be put into explicit mathematical terms.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.910
In the business, you might rephrase this as an enumeration of all proper

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:05.920
subsets of a cube that are connected and span R3 modulo rotations.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.161
If you told the mathematician to find such an enumeration,

00:03:09.161 --> 00:03:11.630
this table is basically what they would come up with.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.030
So we have something interesting.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:18.510
An artist who effectively solved a math problem as part of his creative process.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.950
Who was Sol LeWitt and what was he trying to accomplish with this piece?

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:29.470
Sol LeWitt was a modern artist associated with conceptual art, minimalism, and serial art.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.790
The core idea of conceptual art can be seen in LeWitt's series of wall drawings.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.080
LeWitt would provide a set of instructions for how a wall should be drawn or painted on.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.240
In this case, wall drawing number 118,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.320
he specifies a drawing with 50 points, all connected by lines.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.694
Trained installers would then follow these instructions to

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:50.800
produce the actual piece on the walls of galleries and museums.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.259
Like a musician writing a score to be performed by others,

00:03:54.259 --> 00:03:58.320
LeWitt's instructions emphasize the conceptual nature of the resulting work.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.920
It's the idea of the piece that is the true product of the artist.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.151
The in perhaps his most well known quote, LeWitt said,

00:04:05.151 --> 00:04:07.700
the idea becomes a machine that makes art.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.420
Minimalism is to some degree, exactly what it sounds like.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.100
LeWitt's work uses very minimal ornamentation.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:19.060
Much of it is flat white because, as he put it, white is less expressive than black.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.980
And most of the sculptures are simple geometric forms like the cube.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.093
Of the cube, he wrote, the most interesting characteristic

00:04:26.093 --> 00:04:28.740
of the cube is that it is relatively uninteresting.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.824
Therefore, it is the best form to use for any more elaborate function,

00:04:32.824 --> 00:04:35.740
the grammatical device from which the work may proceed.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.009
In the prior decade, modern art was dominated by Abstract Expressionism,

00:04:40.009 --> 00:04:44.780
famous for paintings filled with splatters and splotches and other acts of randomness.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.140
Serial art was a reaction to this arbitrariness.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.847
LeWitt sought instead a technique in which he said,

00:04:50.847 --> 00:04:53.593
all of the planning and decisions are made beforehand,

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:55.740
and the execution is a perfunctory affair.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:00.090
Many of LeWitt's serial structures begin with the question, how many ways?

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.090
Like how many ways can I put five cubes on 25 squares?

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.530
Or how many ways can I stack three open boxes?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.302
The resulting artworks are simply a presentation

00:05:10.302 --> 00:05:12.570
of the correct answers to these questions.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:17.450
Sometime in 1973, LeWitt asked himself a new serial art style question:

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.890
How many ways can a cube be incomplete?

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.060
And we have something pretty amazing.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.043
A surprisingly intact record of the artist's thought

00:05:26.043 --> 00:05:28.740
process as he went about solving this problem.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.727
Sol LeWitt left behind about 50 notebook pages,

00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:35.620
which show sketches and working drawings that capture the development of

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.060
the incomplete open cubes.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.218
These are currently in the archives at the Wadsworth Athenium in Connecticut,

00:05:41.218 --> 00:05:45.320
who were kind enough to let us examine these drawings and reproduce them here,

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.942
by reviewing their contents, we effectively get to look over Sol LeWitt's shoulder as he

00:05:49.942 --> 00:05:50.980
solves the problem.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.937
One of the things these notebooks make clear is that of the three constraints,

00:05:55.937 --> 00:05:59.030
rotational equivalence was by far the most difficult to figure out.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.733
It's easy to tell at a glance if a cube is flat or disconnected,

00:06:02.733 --> 00:06:07.014
but in page after page of these notebooks, we see LeWitt struggling to eliminate

00:06:07.014 --> 00:06:08.230
rotational duplicates.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:11.081
As the story goes, he would make small models of

00:06:11.081 --> 00:06:13.430
the cubes out of paperclips or pipe cleaners.

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:16.306
By comparing the models, he would occasionally discover that two

00:06:16.306 --> 00:06:19.190
cubes he had thought were distinct turn out to be the same shape.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.740
The red marks indicate times when he found duplicates and had to cross one off.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.256
As LeWitt explained, "I was trying to figure out a

00:06:26.256 --> 00:06:28.540
way to do it through numbers and letters logically.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.860
But in the end, it all had to be done empirically.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:34.140
I had to build a model for each one and then rotate it."

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.105
Although he never found a purely mathematical solution,

00:06:37.105 --> 00:06:40.134
as we'll see from these notebooks, LeWitt did find many clever

00:06:40.134 --> 00:06:43.260
tricks and ingenious simplifications on the way to his solution.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.020
A true thinking process.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:49.293
It was only after he'd completed the piece that LeWitt consulted

00:06:49.293 --> 00:06:52.918
with two mathematicians who confirmed that he'd found the right number.

00:06:52.918 --> 00:06:55.990
While LeWitt had to solve the problem painstakingly by hand,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.636
it seems a mathematician is someone who knows how to pull

00:06:58.636 --> 00:07:01.190
this number out of thin air with a simple calculation.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:05.750
But in the beginning, the mathematician doesn't know anything about the problem either.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:08.594
They, too, must go through some exploratory thought

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:10.790
process in order to figure out the answer.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.600
In this video, we're going to solve the specific problem that eluded Sol LeWitt.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.120
And we're going to do it by showing a thought process that leads to the solution.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.885
A little housekeeping before we begin our mathematical exploration

00:07:22.885 --> 00:07:26.120
is going to focus only on the problem of rotational equivalence.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.320
We'll relax these other constraints for now.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.440
This means that we'll be considering all possible cubes,

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.440
including flat or disconnected ones, and even the full cube and the empty cube.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.000
As a consequence, the number of families we find is going to be larger than LeWitt's

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.140
final count of 122, at least until we bring back the other conditions at the end.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:45.980
Let's begin.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:53.796
Lots of the pages in Sol LeWitt's notebooks are made up of loose sketches

00:07:53.796 --> 00:07:57.500
which show him just kind of exploring the idea of incomplete cubes.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:01.246
Pages in this style don't show much strategy or organization,

00:08:01.246 --> 00:08:06.300
just a cloud of partial cube doodles as he was getting familiar with the general concept.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:10.100
A wonderful document in the collection is this business letter to LeWitt.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.130
If you look closely, you can see that the back of the sheet

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.180
has clearly been used as incomplete open cube scratch paper.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:19.558
Anyone who's ever gotten obsessed with a math problem will find this

00:08:19.558 --> 00:08:22.660
familiar how the problem just starts to get all over your paperwork.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.660
One way for us to begin poking at this problem

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.940
is to notice that there is a simpler version.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.435
Instead of taking on the incomplete open cubes,

00:08:30.435 --> 00:08:32.980
let's warm up by considering the two dimensional case.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.740
What are all of the rotationally equivalent incomplete open squares?

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.495
This is a more bite sized version of the problem,

00:08:39.495 --> 00:08:42.350
one which we'll be able to think through in all of its details.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.303
To begin, let's set aside these constraints and just ask how many squares do

00:08:46.303 --> 00:08:49.950
we have to worry about altogether before we start sorting and filtering them?

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:55.790
To define an incomplete square, each edge presents us with two choices, on or off.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:58.887
And from this sequence of choices we get a tree of

00:08:58.887 --> 00:09:01.630
decisions which generates all possible squares.

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:06.045
For example, if we choose the top edge to be on, the left edge off,

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:09.970
the bottom on and the right on, we get this incomplete square.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.050
Every other outcome is similar.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.142
If we take the side view of our decision tree,

00:09:15.142 --> 00:09:18.530
we can see that the number of possibilities doubles at every level.

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:21.338
So the number of outcomes at the bottom must equal

00:09:21.338 --> 00:09:23.650
2 raised to the height of the decision tree.

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:24.450
That is:

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:26.786
There's a formula we can use for calculating the

00:09:26.786 --> 00:09:29.170
number of possible outcomes of a tree like this.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:33.330
In general, it's 2 raised to the number of choices we have to make.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.586
The beauty of a formula like this is that it makes our life much

00:09:36.586 --> 00:09:39.510
easier when we move on to the more complicated case of the cubes.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:43.110
A cube has 12 edges that can be set to on or off.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:45.510
So we have 12 binary choices to make.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:50.470
2 to the 12th gives us 4096 total incomplete open cubes.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.084
And it's very convenient to be able to just calculate that

00:09:53.084 --> 00:09:55.750
number rather than having to try and figure it out manually.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:00.204
Now let's get down to the main problem of how to sort these 16

00:10:00.204 --> 00:10:03.510
incomplete open squares into rotationally equivalent families.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:05.870
We'll start with this one part square.

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.870
What is the family it belongs to?

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:12.390
If we compare it to its neighbor, we can see that they are rotationally equivalent.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:16.350
That is, by reorienting one, we can get them to look exactly the same.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:20.190
So we gather these together and say they're part of the same family.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.950
This next square is obviously not going to belong to this family.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.924
If we want to be thorough, we can rotate it through its

00:10:26.924 --> 00:10:29.870
possible positions just to be sure, and then reject it.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:32.970
The next one is another member of this family,

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.984
and we just proceed down the whole length in this way,

00:10:35.984 --> 00:10:39.490
scooping up any rotationally equivalent squares we come across.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:44.130
Our one part squares form a rotationally equivalent family of four.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:48.610
And now we go through the list again, this time picking up all the corner pieces.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.490
These also gather into a family of four.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:54.555
Now, if all families had four members, then it would

00:10:54.555 --> 00:10:57.090
be easy to calculate the total number of families.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:01.680
So 16 squares divided into groups of four would mean four families total.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.200
Unfortunately, the parallel bars break this pattern

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.541
by collecting into a family of only two. The three

00:11:08.541 --> 00:11:11.280
part squares form another family of four.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.679
Finally, the complete square is only rotationally equivalent to itself,

00:11:15.679 --> 00:11:17.280
so it forms a family of one.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:18.880
Same for the empty square.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:25.040
And there we are, six total families of rotationally equivalent incomplete open squares.

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.410
We got this result using brute force search.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:32.810
What we'd really like is a formula, something we can also apply to the cube.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:35.501
If we had that mathematical confirmation, then it would

00:11:35.501 --> 00:11:37.970
be easy to then apply Sol Lewitt's other conditions

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:41.226
for the art piece. A representative from each family forms

00:11:41.226 --> 00:11:44.650
a collection of rotationally unique incomplete open squares.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:48.170
We can get rid of the square on the right because it isn't incomplete.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.625
We can get rid of the two squares on the left

00:11:50.625 --> 00:11:52.970
because they aren't properly two dimensional.

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:56.290
And we can get rid of the square in the middle because it isn't connected.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:58.370
That leaves us with these two.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:03.330
And there we have it, the complete variations of incomplete open squares.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:04.370
Beautiful.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:09.220
One of the great things about this piece is that it gives us some appreciation for the

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:13.211
added complexity that seems to arise when we consider the same problem in three

00:12:13.211 --> 00:12:13.810
dimensions.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:17.240
Just for the fun of it, let's think through what it would be like

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:20.130
to try and do a brute force search for families of the cubes.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:26.370
So here we are moving down a hallway that has all 4,096 incomplete open cubes.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:29.106
To sort these cubes into families, you'd have to go

00:12:29.106 --> 00:12:31.570
down the line and manually compare them one by one.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.624
And keep in mind that unlike with squares, cubes are actually

00:12:34.624 --> 00:12:37.090
kind of tricky with regards to rotational equivalents.

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:39.490
You may have to tumble them around for a while to be sure.

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:42.130
So this is going to take some focus and concentration.

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:46.290
It's also going to take a lot of time because this is really a very long hallway.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.745
4096 is one of those numbers where, you know it's big,

00:12:49.745 --> 00:12:54.000
but it's hard to really appreciate how big until you see a hallway like this.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:57.440
In fact, we're going to need to speed things up a little bit to get where we're going.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.397
And as we zoom along here, keep in mind that you'll have to go down this

00:13:01.397 --> 00:13:04.960
hallway over and over again, one time for each family you gather up.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.440
So clearly this is going to be quite an ordeal.

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:12.080
In fact, after all this travel, we've only made it to the halfway point of the hallway.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:16.110
So we've come up with a pretty vivid sense of the difficulty of our problem.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.830
But enough goofing around, let's make a plan.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:22.910
Clearly, Sol LeWitt has his work cut out for him. To make any progress,

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:26.350
he's probably going to have to come up with something better than brute force search.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:31.023
When you're comparing two cubes to see if they might be rotationally equivalent,

00:13:31.023 --> 00:13:34.350
a good question to ask is: do they have the same number of edges?

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:38.021
A five part cube and a six part cube will never look the same,

00:13:38.021 --> 00:13:39.710
no matter how we position them.

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:42.878
From early on, we see Lewitt restricting his attention

00:13:42.878 --> 00:13:44.690
to cubes with a given number of edges.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.580
Different pages dedicated to different sized cubes show how

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:52.210
he broke the overall problem into several smaller problems.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:56.690
This divide and conquer approach is reflected in the final layout of the art piece.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.890
The bottom row contains all possible three part cubes.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:04.770
The next row contains all of the four part cubes, and so on, row by row up the table.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:08.342
By breaking the overall problem into nine smaller problems,

00:14:08.342 --> 00:14:12.700
LeWitt makes the first of several simplifications that we'll see him bring to the task.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:14.220
What about us?

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.180
We're hoping to come up with a formula.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:19.260
So what kind of strategy should we consider to attack the problem?

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:23.294
When we found the decision tree formula, it was by considering the

00:14:23.294 --> 00:14:26.860
decision process itself and then noticing a formula that described it.

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.056
If we want to do the same thing for sorting the shapes into families,

00:14:30.056 --> 00:14:32.540
then we probably want to study the associated process.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:36.179
That is, while Sol LeWitt is trying to get away from brute force search,

00:14:36.179 --> 00:14:39.530
for us, it might make sense to actually look directly at this process,

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.970
and see if we can understand what it is accomplishing.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.520
Obviously, it's a method that's going to take a while,

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.770
but if we did have all the time in the world, then it would be perfectly straightforward.

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:52.431
We just pick up one of the cubes, travel the length of the hallway,

00:14:52.431 --> 00:14:57.130
gathering up its rotational equivalents, and that's one family. Moving down the line,

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.929
we pick up the next cube and gather up its family,

00:14:59.929 --> 00:15:02.280
and the next one gives us another family.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.325
And then once more, we find that some cubes seem

00:15:05.325 --> 00:15:07.480
to belong to smaller families than others.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:10.955
It's important to appreciate that this irregularity

00:15:10.955 --> 00:15:13.400
is what's making our counting problem hard.

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.116
If every family were the same size, then it would

00:15:16.116 --> 00:15:18.360
be easy to calculate the total number of families.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.946
But if every family seems to be doing its own thing in terms of size,

00:15:21.946 --> 00:15:26.117
then we apparently have no choice but to go through and manually count them up like

00:15:26.117 --> 00:15:27.160
this, one at a time.

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:31.800
Before we can make any progress on our main question of how many families there are,

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.810
it seems like we first want to understand a more local problem.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:38.650
For a given incomplete open cube, how big is its family?

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:41.950
For instance, if I gave you a cube shaped like a table,

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:44.490
could you reason about how big its family must be?

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.010
Try looking at it this way.

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.810
The table has one complete square side on top.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:52.530
A cube has six sides, total, six directions

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.650
the tabletop could be oriented to point.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.850
Based on this logic, we would expect a family size of six.

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.360
And that is indeed what we find.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:01.918
Find if we try this with a few different cubes,

00:16:01.918 --> 00:16:05.299
we'll quickly notice that the ones that belong to large 24 member

00:16:05.299 --> 00:16:09.910
families tend to be a bit more irregular looking, like this weird character on the right.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.759
While the shapes like the table that are more orderly

00:16:12.759 --> 00:16:15.270
or symmetrical seem to belong to smaller families.

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:16.950
This makes sense in a way.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:22.070
If a cube is oddly shaped, then it probably looks different in each possible orientation.

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:23.990
So this is a kind of hunch

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:28.150
we might develop, that more symmetrical shapes seem to have smaller families.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:32.590
For Sol LeWitt, the best plan seems to be divide and conquer.

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:37.328
For us, the plan is to delay the main question and first focus on a simpler question,

00:16:37.328 --> 00:16:39.710
how to calculate a given cube's family size.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:43.470
And we'll pursue our hunch that this has something to do with symmetry.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.256
Both approaches still have a lot of work to do,

00:16:46.256 --> 00:16:49.950
but we each now have a strategy for how we're going to dig into this problem.

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:54.270
In LeWitt's notebooks, we see him devise a number of different label concepts.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.240
He came up with a numbering system for the cubes themselves.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.708
Two integers separated by a slash in which the first number describes how many

00:17:01.708 --> 00:17:05.720
parts the cube has, and the second part is an arbitrarily assigned index.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:10.170
Obviously, the cubes don't come in any specific order, but by giving each a number,

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:14.200
Lewitt was able to bring some orderliness and consistency to his investigations.

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:18.920
He also experimented with a couple of different ways of labeling the parts of the cube.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:23.080
In one, he assigned letters to the corners, and in another, he numbered the edges.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.760
Which of these do you think is a better notation?

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.022
In LeWitt's case, he started with the corner labels,

00:17:29.022 --> 00:17:31.697
but after creating extensive notes in this style,

00:17:31.697 --> 00:17:34.960
he abandoned this system and switched to the numbered edges.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:39.360
As we'll see, this turns out to be a more efficient and elegant way to annotate the cubes.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:43.760
Moreover, good notation can also help you think about the problem in new ways.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.240
Consider an incomplete open cube.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.811
If we imagine the set of edges that are missing,

00:17:48.811 --> 00:17:52.560
we can then imagine an incomplete open cube made of just those edges.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:56.700
That is, every incomplete open cube is part of a complementary pair.

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:01.260
The edge numbering notation makes it easy to think about complementary pairs.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:05.231
By taking a given cube's label and literally counting off the missing numbers,

00:18:05.231 --> 00:18:07.420
we can obtain the label of its complement.

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:11.389
It was only after LeWitt switched to this edge numbering system that we

00:18:11.389 --> 00:18:15.500
suddenly see him begin to explore cubes and their complements at the same time.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:18.900
And these combinations are useful to consider because the number

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:22.060
of edges of a cube and its complement must always add up to 12.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.760
For example, every four part cube has an eight part complement.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:30.517
Thus, LeWitt could basically explore four and eight part cubes in parallel,

00:18:30.517 --> 00:18:33.760
similarly for five and seven part cubes, and so on.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:38.720
By exploiting this duality, LeWitt was cleverly able to cut his search effort in half.

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:43.680
Finding the right labeling system can be an invaluable step in making sense of a problem.

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:47.520
So what's something that might be useful for us to label?

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.930
We're interested in understanding families such as this 24 member group.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.850
And currently we don't have any way of referring to specific family members.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:58.810
So this could be a good candidate for a new labeling system.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:01.808
For instance, what is the relationship between the

00:19:01.808 --> 00:19:04.490
given cube and this family member in the lower corner?

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.366
We can see that if we spin the given cube by 90 degrees,

00:19:08.366 --> 00:19:11.370
we obtain the orientation shown by that lower cube.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:14.948
We might even think to label that as the cube we get when we

00:19:14.948 --> 00:19:18.480
rotate the given cube by 90 degrees around the vertical axis.

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:23.590
A similar kind of geometric description could be applied to all of these family members.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.755
And we can work out this labeling system by just thinking

00:19:26.755 --> 00:19:29.350
about all the different ways of reorienting a cube.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:34.310
For instance, we've seen that we can rotate the cube 90 degrees around the vertical axis.

00:19:34.549 --> 00:19:38.199
But we can also keep spinning to 180 degrees and 270

00:19:38.199 --> 00:19:41.990
degrees before returning to the original orientation.

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:45.859
So let's take our given cube and make three copies

00:19:45.859 --> 00:19:48.310
and apply each of these transformations.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:53.580
The result is three different orientations of the cube, all distinct from one another.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:58.140
The vertical axis runs through two opposite faces of the cube.

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:03.900
There are two other face centered axes we might call our set f1, f2 and f3.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:08.700
And each one of these can be used to give three new orientations of the cube.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:16.220
In total, we get nine additional family members by rotating around these axes.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:20.428
Let's zoom in on the first example and in the spirit of Sol LeWitt,

00:20:20.428 --> 00:20:21.980
we'll give it a two part label.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:27.100
f1 tells us the axis and 90 degrees tells us how much to rotate.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:31.260
We can apply this kind of label to all of the family members shown here.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.692
Another axis we can twirl the cube around is this

00:20:34.692 --> 00:20:37.740
diagonal line that passes through opposite corners.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:41.670
We can call it a corner axis and see that we can

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:45.180
spin a cube 120 degrees or 240 degrees around it.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:49.690
Applying this to a pair of copies, we find two new orientations.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:55.330
If we work our way around the cube, we'll find a total of four axes like this,

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:57.130
which we can label c1 to c4.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:00.970
Those first two examples we saw were the rotations around c1.

00:21:01.369 --> 00:21:03.930
And here are the outputs of all the rest.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:10.186
And at last, it is also possible to run an axis between

00:21:10.186 --> 00:21:13.530
diagonal edges and give the cube a 180 degree spin.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:19.540
The 12 edges of the cube pair off to make six of these axes, which we'll label like this.

00:21:20.260 --> 00:21:24.180
Here's a new cube orientation we can get from spinning around e1.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:26.340
And here are the rest.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:31.332
With six cubes from the edge axes, eight from the corner axes and nine

00:21:31.332 --> 00:21:35.620
from the face axes, we end up with 23 distinct orientations of the cube.

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:39.571
If we include the original cube, to which we did Nothing,

00:21:39.571 --> 00:21:44.160
we get 24 distinct orientations, 24 uniquely labeled family members.

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:48.240
There are simpler ways to deduce that there must be 24 rotations.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:52.582
But what we'll find later is that having an explicit list of all 24

00:21:52.582 --> 00:21:56.640
will be crucial for our final step in counting incomplete cubes.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.360
Note that what we've really labeled are actions.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:03.360
A label like 120 degree rotation around c4 refers to a

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:07.280
transformation that can be applied to any given cube.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:12.072
If we grab a random incomplete open cube, we can supply it as input to the

00:22:12.072 --> 00:22:16.600
transformation and get a generally different incomplete open cube as output.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:21.381
If we take this same input and supply it to all 24 of our transformations,

00:22:21.381 --> 00:22:25.880
we get all 24 orientations of that shape that is all of its family members.

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:29.229
We effectively have a recipe for taking any incomplete

00:22:29.229 --> 00:22:32.280
open cube and generating a copy of its whole family.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:34.760
We'll call this a family portrait.

00:22:35.390 --> 00:22:38.007
But what do you think would happen if we made the family

00:22:38.007 --> 00:22:40.670
portrait of a shape that doesn't have 24 family members?

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:44.910
For instance, we know this table looking cube only has six members in its family.

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:48.910
So if we make its family portrait, what's going to go in the other 18 slots?

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:52.990
One thing we can say for sure is that there are going to be repeats.

00:22:53.390 --> 00:22:57.250
The reason for this is that we're about to knock over a table 24 different ways,

00:22:57.250 --> 00:22:59.470
but there are only six ways a table can land.

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:02.910
So let's apply the transformations and see what we get.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:07.020
There are definitely a lot of repeats, perhaps most noticeably here in the upper corner.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:10.112
In addition to the unchanged cube, there are three

00:23:10.112 --> 00:23:12.940
manipulated cubes that end up still being upright.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.500
We'll color code the upright tables green.

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.460
Now consider these four transformations.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.740
Each one manages to knock the table into the upside down position.

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:24.700
We'll color code upside down tables purple.

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:28.780
And we can see that our portrait then contains four tables in this orientation.

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:31.663
In fact, it turns out there are four ways to get

00:23:31.663 --> 00:23:33.660
a table pointing in any particular direction.

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.940
Which means we get exactly four copies of every family member.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.432
Another way to say this is that we can take this family portrait and we can

00:23:41.432 --> 00:23:45.260
rearrange the pieces into four complete copies of our six member family.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.300
So we're seeing some structure here.

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:51.740
It's not just all upside down tables or alternating left and right tables.

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:54.260
The family portrait worked like a copy machine.

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:56.620
It made four complete replicas of this family.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:59.080
Does this happen with other small families?

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:02.760
In this three part cube, the edges all meet at one corner.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:07.120
A complete cube has eight corners that a triple like this could be clustered around.

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:09.992
And so we can reason that there are eight distinct

00:24:09.992 --> 00:24:12.520
orientations for this incomplete open cube.

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:14.800
So now let's make a family portrait.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.600
We'll use this color coding to identify family members in the output.

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:22.049
After we apply all the transformations, we discover that once

00:24:22.049 --> 00:24:24.920
again we have gotten complete copies of the entire family.

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:26.600
This time a total of three.

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:30.360
Let's compare these with the copies from the previous example.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.920
What can we say about these rectangles?

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.200
For one thing, they both have 24 squares in total.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.080
Because that's how many cubes are in a family portrait.

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:42.920
The height of these rectangles is the specific thing we're interested in.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.800
The size of the family that a given shape belongs to.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:47.800
What about across the top?

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:52.050
What can we say about the family members that are giving width to these rectangles?

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:56.019
In the first case, we see the familiar set of tables

00:24:56.019 --> 00:24:58.450
which look unchanged by their transformations.

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:00.690
In the second case, we see something similar.

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:05.090
All the corner pieces which ended up in the portrait looking just like they did before.

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:09.330
We're going to call these cubes in the family portrait lookalikes.

00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:13.090
To be more specific, consider any cube and any transformation.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:16.424
A lookalike is what results if the cube looks the same

00:25:16.424 --> 00:25:19.250
after applying the transformation as it did before.

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:24.380
In the examples from before, the cubes we did nothing to will always be lookalikes.

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:27.115
But families may have additional lookalikes, and

00:25:27.115 --> 00:25:29.660
these add additional width to our rectangles.

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:32.780
This shows us that there seems to be a numerical relationship.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:35.564
The size of a given family times the number of

00:25:35.564 --> 00:25:38.620
lookalikes in its family portrait always equals 24.

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:42.515
To put it another way, it seems we can calculate how big a cube's

00:25:42.515 --> 00:25:46.140
family is by counting its lookalikes and dividing 24 by that number.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:47.570
Let's give it a try.

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:50.210
Consider this cube with one edge removed.

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:51.570
How big is its family?

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:55.356
In order to use our formula, we need to think of all the ways

00:25:55.356 --> 00:25:58.530
to manipulate this cube that leave it apparently unchanged.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.330
One option is always to do nothing to it.

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:05.810
But we can also spin the cube around this edge axis and it stays the same as before.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:10.290
The cube will look the same as long as that bottom left edge stays in place.

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:14.050
And it turns out there are no other transformations that leave it fixed.

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:16.040
So that gives us two lookalikes total.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:18.760
And that implies that the family size is 12.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:23.400
We can actually corroborate this result by remembering that a cube has 12 edges.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:27.480
So there should be 12 distinct ways to remove a single edge from a cube.

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:29.080
So the formula checks out.

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:32.680
The formula also seems to apply in our various edge cases.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:35.800
Let's make the family portrait of a complete cube.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.432
Of course, this turns out to be nothing but lookalikes,

00:26:38.432 --> 00:26:40.680
since a cube looks the same from every direction.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:44.033
But according to our formula, 24 divided by 24 is 1,

00:26:44.033 --> 00:26:47.370
and the cube is indeed the only member of its family.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.050
Suppose we have another family portrait.

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:54.170
This one contains no lookalikes other than the unchanged cube at the top.

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:57.669
24 divided by this minimum number of lookalikes

00:26:57.669 --> 00:27:00.570
ends up with the maximum family size of 24.

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:04.188
So we have found a formulaic answer to our question,

00:27:04.188 --> 00:27:07.050
and in a way, we've confirmed our hunch about symmetry.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:09.976
To say that a shape is symmetrical is really to say

00:27:09.976 --> 00:27:12.440
that it looks the same from various points of view.

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:16.320
The number of lookalikes is basically a numerical measure of symmetry.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:20.200
And our formula captures how more symmetry means smaller families.

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:24.600
Conceptually, we now know families and lookalikes are related ideas.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.844
Consider that in order to notice this relationship,

00:27:27.844 --> 00:27:31.560
we had to be able to ask "what happens if we take the family portrait of a table?"

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:35.880
And it's impossible to even ask this question until we use our labeling system to define.

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:37.710
what a family portrait is.

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:41.470
A good labeling system does more than just organize your data.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:46.386
Much as LeWitt's edge labels may have guided him to thinking about complementary pairs,

00:27:46.386 --> 00:27:49.773
our labeling system has enabled us to find a hidden connection

00:27:49.773 --> 00:27:52.030
between two seemingly unrelated concepts.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.646
Now, as we turn our attention back to the bigger problem of

00:27:55.646 --> 00:27:58.606
determining the total number of families, we can come at this

00:27:58.606 --> 00:28:01.470
question knowing that lookalikes may turn out to be useful.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:06.881
Another kind of scribbling you can find throughout the notebooks are

00:28:06.881 --> 00:28:10.360
running totals for the number of cubes Lewitt had found at a given time.

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:14.193
Here, for instance, he's adding up the counts for three part,

00:28:14.193 --> 00:28:17.400
four part, five part cubes, and so on for a total of 79.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:20.200
He then tries to divide 79 by 12.

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:21.160
Why?

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:22.920
Here's my theory.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:26.503
In any sensible universe, the total number of incomplete open cubes

00:28:26.503 --> 00:28:30.320
should have some numerical relationship with the the geometry of a cube.

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:32.240
A cube has 12 sides.

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:34.815
So I propose that LeWitt was dividing his total

00:28:34.815 --> 00:28:37.280
by 12 to see if it happens to come out even.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:41.602
I just love this because it's such a common part of struggling with a math

00:28:41.602 --> 00:28:46.000
problem where you're just kind of blindly but optimistically probing for patterns.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:50.280
Conceptual artists are mystics rather than rationalists, LeWitt wrote.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:53.360
They leap to conclusions that logic cannot reach.

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:57.755
In a similar way, mathematical problem solving can often involve phases

00:28:57.755 --> 00:29:01.860
where we just experimentally combine different ideas in search of patterns.

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:04.650
For example, let's take this incomplete open cube

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:07.220
and begin playing around with its family portrait.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:11.011
Now, presumably, we could create this family portrait

00:29:11.011 --> 00:29:13.700
using any of these family members as a starting point.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:15.220
For example, this cube here.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:17.829
Let's pull this one out and generate a new family

00:29:17.829 --> 00:29:20.100
portrait based on its various orientations.

00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:23.300
What can we say about these two family portraits?

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:26.020
First of all, they depict the same family.

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:29.940
Any member you can find on the left can also be found somewhere on the right.

00:29:30.260 --> 00:29:32.260
But the portraits are also clearly different.

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:35.124
In order to compare them, let's stack them up

00:29:35.124 --> 00:29:37.940
vertically and take a look at each entry side by side.

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:40.420
We know that these top two are different.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:44.340
We chose them as distinct family members when we created these portraits.

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:48.420
These two show the result of applying a 90 degree rotation.

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:53.540
But clearly two different cubes will remain different after a 90 degree twist,

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:58.500
and they remain different when they are both turned by 180 degrees and so on.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:03.634
So we can see that while these two columns have complete agreement about their overall

00:30:03.634 --> 00:30:08.208
contents, they necessarily have zero agreement about which family members actually go

00:30:08.208 --> 00:30:08.580
where.

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:10.740
So we can make an observation:

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:15.620
Different portraits of the same family are just scrambled versions of each other.

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:19.620
There is a unique family portrait generated by each member of the family.

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:24.494
And so we can bring in all 24 family members, generate their 24 distinct family

00:30:24.494 --> 00:30:28.231
portraits, and then pile these portraits into one big collection,

00:30:28.231 --> 00:30:30.100
which we'll call a family album.

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:33.060
Now, this is obviously a pretty abstract idea.

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.860
Is there anything interesting we can say about this grid?

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.573
We know that lookalikes are a relevant idea, so we could ask,

00:30:39.573 --> 00:30:42.020
how do lookalikes show up in family albums?

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:45.382
I would say that this question is kind of our version of

00:30:45.382 --> 00:30:48.820
dividing by 12 in that we may not be sure what we're looking for

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:49.380
exactly.

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:53.592
Guided more by intuition than logic, we're just going to put these ideas of

00:30:53.592 --> 00:30:57.450
family albums and lookalikes together and see if anything interesting happens.

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:01.370
And it turns out that if we give it a little thought, there is a pattern we can find.

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:05.290
In particular, we're looking at a full sized family of 24.

00:31:05.529 --> 00:31:08.970
So each of these columns is only going to contain a single lookalike.

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:13.860
What's going to be different in a family album where a column has two lookalikes,

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:14.650
first of all?

00:31:14.650 --> 00:31:17.850
Well, the column on the left has 24 distinct family members.

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:21.812
The column on the right is going to contain two full copies of the family,

00:31:21.812 --> 00:31:23.840
which therefore can only have 12 members.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.360
So when we go to produce the rest of the family album, we're only going to get 12 columns.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:34.213
But if the first column has two lookalikes, and if all the columns are just scrambled

00:31:34.213 --> 00:31:38.960
versions of each other, then all 12 of these columns should contain two lookalikes each.

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:42.392
So although these two albums have different sizes,

00:31:42.392 --> 00:31:46.010
their overall count of lookalikes should be the same, 24 each.

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:50.490
A similar kind of rebalancing would happen with any number of lookalikes.

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:56.010
3 lookalikes means 1/3 as many columns, but 3 times as many lookalikes per column.

00:31:56.410 --> 00:31:59.118
4 lookalikes means a quarter as many columns, but

00:31:59.118 --> 00:32:01.770
quadruple the density of lookalikes per column.

00:32:02.090 --> 00:32:04.797
That is, in theory at least, we're predicting that

00:32:04.797 --> 00:32:07.450
every family album should contain 24 lookalikes.

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:09.530
Let's see how this looks in practice.

00:32:10.010 --> 00:32:14.201
To generate this family's album, we make a column of copies of each member,

00:32:14.201 --> 00:32:15.710
apply our transformations,

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:20.270
and we indeed see four lookalikes distributed in each of the six columns.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:23.350
We can do the same thing with the three part corner piece.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:26.510
Here we see eight columns with three lookalikes each.

00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:30.033
And in the case of the cubes with a single missing edge,

00:32:30.033 --> 00:32:32.750
we get 12 columns with two lookalikes per column.

00:32:32.910 --> 00:32:37.390
In all cases, this construction gives a total of 24 lookalikes per family.

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:40.881
If we ignore everything but the lookalikes, we can arrange

00:32:40.881 --> 00:32:43.710
them into columns and begin building up a new rectangle

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:50.510
piling up a column from each family. So its width is the total number of families.

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:52.990
The height of this rectangle is 24.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:58.590
So we could say that the number of families times 24 gives the total number of lookalikes.

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:02.913
Putting this another way, if we counted up all the lookalikes and divided by 24,

00:33:02.913 --> 00:33:05.150
this would give us the number of families.

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:08.510
And technically, this is what we've been looking for the whole time.

00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:10.990
A formula that outputs the number of families.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:13.520
Could we actually solve the problem this way?

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:18.000
As usual, we can turn to the two dimensional case to see this technique in action.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:22.800
So here are the six families of incomplete open squares.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:27.440
Our plan basically is to go through each of these and generate its family album.

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:30.157
Then we'll count up all the lookalikes this produces

00:33:30.157 --> 00:33:32.960
and then we'll see how that number works in our formula.

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:35.440
Let's start with the family of elbows.

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:39.720
In the 2D case, there are only four possible transformations we can apply.

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:45.580
We can do nothing, and we can rotate by 90 degrees, 180 and 270 degrees.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:49.980
So the family portrait for this square involves four transformed copies.

00:33:50.140 --> 00:33:54.220
Notice that the only lookalike in this column is the unchanged entry up at the top.

00:33:56.380 --> 00:33:59.624
Pulling out the other four portraits to produce our family album,

00:33:59.624 --> 00:34:01.820
we ultimately find four lookalikes overall.

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:06.060
Let's compare this to the album produced by the family of parallel bars.

00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:09.299
Unpacking these, we find two lookalikes in each column,

00:34:09.299 --> 00:34:11.769
once again giving four lookalikes overall.

00:34:12.169 --> 00:34:14.699
So, in 2D, the general rule appears to be that each

00:34:14.699 --> 00:34:17.129
family contributes four lookalikes to the total.

00:34:18.329 --> 00:34:21.010
If we bring in all six families and unpack them,

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:24.809
we can see that there is a grand total of 24 lookalikes altogether.

00:34:25.209 --> 00:34:27.998
Now, when we have all of the families sorted out like this,

00:34:27.998 --> 00:34:31.449
it's easy to see that each one contributes four lookalikes to our total.

00:34:31.529 --> 00:34:35.180
But the power of our formula becomes apparent when we remember that

00:34:35.180 --> 00:34:38.830
these incomplete open squares were first presented to us unsorted.

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:41.950
This is the order of the squares we originally started with.

00:34:42.190 --> 00:34:45.746
In this state, we didn't know which squares belong to which families,

00:34:45.746 --> 00:34:48.117
but each column is the same as it was before,

00:34:48.117 --> 00:34:51.364
and the number of yellow squares is the same regardless of the

00:34:51.364 --> 00:34:52.910
arrangement of these columns.

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:55.150
So we can just count these off directly.

00:34:55.390 --> 00:35:00.430
The first column has four lookalikes, the next one has just one, and so on down the line.

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:03.740
Adding these up, we find 24 total look alikes,

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:06.550
and we know that each family contributes four.

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:09.350
So that tells us there must be six families in here.

00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:11.830
And this is exactly what we were looking for.

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.294
We've been able to calculate that there must be six

00:35:14.294 --> 00:35:16.710
families without having to do any actual sorting.

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:21.591
Notice that in this approach, we have no idea which squares belong to which families or

00:35:21.591 --> 00:35:26.310
how big any of the families are, only that there must be six families in there somewhere.

00:35:27.190 --> 00:35:30.598
Exciting though this may seem, we are in for some disappointment.

00:35:30.598 --> 00:35:33.220
When we try to apply this technique to our cubes,

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:35.860
we will quickly discover that it doesn't really help us.

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:40.740
First of all, it's not generally easier to count lookalikes than family size.

00:35:40.900 --> 00:35:43.700
And furthermore, we're still going to have to go down this darn hallway.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:47.177
So it's kind of cute that this approach would work technically,

00:35:47.177 --> 00:35:50.020
but it's pretty useless from a practical perspective.

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:54.898
All we've really shown is that the number of families, which we know is hard to count,

00:35:54.898 --> 00:35:58.820
is related to this other thing, lookalikes, which are also hard to count.

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:04.440
Disappointment is part of doing business. Like Sol LeWitt dividing by 12,

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:08.640
you should never be afraid to stir the food around on the plate and see what happens.

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:10.400
Sometimes it's a waste of time.

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:13.451
Sometimes you discover something useless but interesting,

00:36:13.451 --> 00:36:16.240
like the relationship between families and lookalikes.

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:20.690
And sometimes the disappointment is just the precursor to a different,

00:36:20.690 --> 00:36:24.000
almost opposite emotion, the euphoria of an epiphany.

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:28.250
Unfortunately for us, you can't really arrange for an epiphany.

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:30.090
They just kind of happen sometimes.

00:36:30.330 --> 00:36:33.337
For instance, maybe you're thinking about something else,

00:36:33.337 --> 00:36:37.347
but you're also noticing the grid of squares out of the corner of your eye,

00:36:37.347 --> 00:36:40.090
and your vision idly traces these horizontal lines.

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:43.850
And now you're sort of gazing at all these look alikes you wish you could count.

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:46.191
And then all of a Sudden you're like, wait a minute,

00:36:46.191 --> 00:36:48.090
I don't have to count these up going this way.

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:50.490
I can also count them up going this way.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:54.264
And before we proceed, I just want to take a minute and describe

00:36:54.264 --> 00:36:56.980
what it can feel like when a thought like this occurs to you.

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:00.591
Before you even have a chance to think it through in any detail,

00:37:00.591 --> 00:37:03.780
some part of you knows that you have just solved the problem.

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:07.336
There's still a bit of work to do, but from this point forward,

00:37:07.336 --> 00:37:11.096
the feeling of the problem seems to change, as if you're now just pushing on

00:37:11.096 --> 00:37:11.780
an open door.

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:15.460
So we're just going to count up the lookalikes going row by row.

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:19.060
How many squares look the same after a 0 degree rotation?

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:19.790
Easy.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:20.830
All 16 of them.

00:37:21.230 --> 00:37:22.990
How about a 90 degree rotation?

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:26.030
Only two, the empty square and the complete square.

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:30.750
At 180 degrees we get four, including the two sets of parallel bars.

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:33.564
And 270 degrees is the same as 90 degrees, giving

00:37:33.564 --> 00:37:36.110
only the empty square and the complete square.

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:40.310
So this is an alternative way of counting up the total of 24 lookalikes.

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:44.030
And it's already a big improvement since we did it in four steps instead of 16.

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:47.550
But let's think a little more carefully about what we're really doing here.

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:52.164
A lookalike is really a combination between a specific cube and a specific

00:37:52.164 --> 00:37:57.124
transformation, such that the cube looks the same after the transformation is applied as

00:37:57.124 --> 00:37:57.960
it did before.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:02.471
We're trying to count up all possible combinations that make this happen,

00:38:02.471 --> 00:38:05.673
and our approach had been to look at each incomplete open cube

00:38:05.673 --> 00:38:08.520
and ask how many transformations make it look the same.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:12.505
Our epiphany was basically that we can count to the same number by

00:38:12.505 --> 00:38:16.222
instead looking at each transformation and asking how many incomplete

00:38:16.222 --> 00:38:19.620
open cubes are left unchanged by this transform transformation.

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:22.097
This turns out to be a fundamentally different

00:38:22.097 --> 00:38:24.300
kind of question that is much easier to answer

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:28.260
in general. As we'll see, it can basically be done with a formula.

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:32.349
To figure out how, let's go back to the 2D case and see if we can

00:38:32.349 --> 00:38:36.963
figure out why it is that a 90 degree rotation only leaves two shapes looking unchanged,

00:38:36.963 --> 00:38:39.140
the empty square and the complete square.

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:42.580
Consider an undetermined incomplete open square.

00:38:43.060 --> 00:38:44.820
What is required for this to be a lookalike

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:49.368
under a 90 degree degree rotation? We're looking for patterns of edges which,

00:38:49.368 --> 00:38:54.270
if we make a copy and rotate it 90 degrees, look the same as before, edge by edge.

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:56.930
Let's imagine, for instance, that we choose the

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:59.470
top edge to be on and leave the rest undetermined.

00:38:59.790 --> 00:39:04.106
After rotating a copy and comparing, we See that in order for these to be the same,

00:39:04.106 --> 00:39:07.070
the original square must have its right edge on as well.

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:11.923
But if the top edge being on forces the right edge to be on, then by the same logic,

00:39:11.923 --> 00:39:16.370
the right edge forces the bottom edge to be on and that forces the left edge to be on.

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:19.759
If instead we had originally chosen the top edge to be off,

00:39:19.759 --> 00:39:22.850
the sequence of other edges would have all been off as well.

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:26.930
Whatever choice we make, it is contagious across all sides of the square.

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:31.890
So lookalikes for a 90 degree rotation are determined by a single overall choice.

00:39:32.050 --> 00:39:33.890
All on or all off.

00:39:34.610 --> 00:39:37.526
Compare this with the 180 degree rotation, which

00:39:37.526 --> 00:39:41.050
basically splits the square into two independent subsets.

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:44.240
The two horizontal edges get exchanged by the rotation.

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:47.993
In order to appear unchanged, they would need to be the same,

00:39:47.993 --> 00:39:50.880
either both on or both off. Entirely separately,

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:55.920
the vertical edges are also exchanged and also must be both on or both off.

00:39:56.480 --> 00:40:00.320
So in this case we have two choices that specify the possible lookalikes.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:03.074
We can represent these choices as a tree in which we

00:40:03.074 --> 00:40:06.040
first decide whether or not there are horizontal sides,

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:10.420
and then for each of those outcomes, we further decide whether there are vertical sides.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:14.900
The bottom row consists of the four lookalikes for the 180 degree rotation.

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:18.331
And from our corresponding formula, we can also calculate

00:40:18.331 --> 00:40:21.060
this number as 2 raised to the second power or 4.

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.900
In some ways, the three dimensional case is a little easier to visualize.

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:27.780
Consider a 90 degree rotation of a cube.

00:40:27.860 --> 00:40:31.515
In this case, the top four edges of the cube get exchanged amongst

00:40:31.515 --> 00:40:35.170
each other and so they must all be the same on or off separately.

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:39.370
The four vertical bars all get exchanged and therefore must be the same as each other.

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:41.890
And finally, the bottom edges must all be the same.

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:44.793
So we basically have three independent choices to make,

00:40:44.793 --> 00:40:48.330
which means we have two to the third or eight possible lookalikes.

00:40:48.410 --> 00:40:50.796
We can show these choices as a decision tree, where

00:40:50.796 --> 00:40:53.370
the first choice is whether a cube has the top square.

00:40:53.450 --> 00:40:55.931
For each of those, whether it has the vertical bars,

00:40:55.931 --> 00:40:58.650
and for each of those, whether it has the bottom square.

00:40:59.050 --> 00:41:01.934
If we run down the final line and give each of these a twirl,

00:41:01.934 --> 00:41:06.000
we see that we have generated all of the incomplete complete open cubes that look the

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:07.560
same after a 90 degree rotation.

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.469
So we know that there are eight look alikes associated

00:41:11.469 --> 00:41:13.160
with this particular transformation.

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:16.221
Our plan for finally solving this problem is to make

00:41:16.221 --> 00:41:19.000
the same assessment for each of our 24 transformations.

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:22.240
And we're already further along than you might think. By symmetry,

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:25.185
the other 90 degree and 270 degree rotations in this

00:41:25.185 --> 00:41:27.960
row will have a similar set of eight lookalikes.

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:32.170
So now we just need to think about the 180 degree rotations in this row.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:35.770
Let's consider the partition of the cube from the previous example.

00:41:36.090 --> 00:41:39.384
Recall how in the 2D case, the 180 degree rotation

00:41:39.384 --> 00:41:42.810
splits the square into two pairs of parallel sides.

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:44.410
A similar thing happens here.

00:41:44.410 --> 00:41:47.641
The top square gets split into two separate subsets,

00:41:47.641 --> 00:41:50.250
each unaffected by a 180 degree rotation.

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:53.370
And the other layers get similarly divided into pairs.

00:41:53.770 --> 00:41:58.710
So overall, this results in six independent choices, suggesting 64 lookalikes.

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:00.070
You can see a set here.

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:03.390
These are all of the incomplete open cubes that look the

00:42:03.390 --> 00:42:06.630
same after a 180 degrees spin around the vertical axis.

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:11.950
This allows us to close out the row of face axes with an additional 64

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:16.470
lookalikes for each of the 180 degree rotations. For the corner axis,

00:42:16.470 --> 00:42:19.990
it's a little easier to see what we're doing if we tilt the axis up vertically.

00:42:20.310 --> 00:42:22.939
Up at the top there's a cluster of three edges which

00:42:22.939 --> 00:42:25.670
all get exchanged and therefore must all be the same.

00:42:26.190 --> 00:42:29.230
There's a similar set of three edges meeting at the bottom corner.

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:31.710
And then we have this ring around the center.

00:42:31.870 --> 00:42:36.510
This divides into these three sides which all get exchanged and must all be the same.

00:42:36.590 --> 00:42:39.230
And these three sides, which also must be the same.

00:42:39.550 --> 00:42:42.380
Altogether that's four distinct choices, giving

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:45.390
16 possible on-off combinations such as this set.

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:48.350
So we get to add a row of 16s to our table.

00:42:49.070 --> 00:42:53.230
Finally, let's tilt the edge axis vertically and work our way from top to bottom.

00:42:53.630 --> 00:42:55.390
We've got the top edge by itself.

00:42:55.710 --> 00:42:57.550
Below that we find a pair of Vs.

00:42:57.630 --> 00:42:59.550
These swap opposite arms.

00:42:59.630 --> 00:43:02.110
So these two edges get exchanged and must be the same.

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:05.070
While these two edges also get swapped and must be the same.

00:43:05.470 --> 00:43:08.190
Below that we have a pair of edges at the midline.

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:12.030
Below that is another pair of Vs which exchange opposite arms.

00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:14.830
And finally the fixed edge at the bottom.

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:19.051
This gives a grand total of seven independent choices for lookalikes

00:43:19.051 --> 00:43:22.900
fixed by the edge axis rotation, meaning 128 distinct shapes.

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:27.620
And after we fill in our final row, we can't forget that the act of

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:31.300
doing nothing contributes a total of 4096 lookalikes to our total.

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:35.892
So we've calculated the number of lookalikes for each transformation,

00:43:35.892 --> 00:43:37.380
and now we just add it all up.

00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:43.460
Ultimately we find 5,232 total lookalikes.

00:43:43.460 --> 00:43:45.860
Dividing by 24 gives us our final answer.

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:51.970
It's seems that there are 218 rotationally unique families of incomplete open cubes.

00:43:52.130 --> 00:43:56.130
And this number is the happy conclusion of our mathematical train of thought.

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:59.053
It's difficult to know for sure what kind of epiphanies might

00:43:59.053 --> 00:44:01.570
have occurred to LeWitt while he was solving this problem.

00:44:01.969 --> 00:44:05.490
But the notes do contain evidence of a possible flash of insight.

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:08.333
Consider the overall organization of the art piece,

00:44:08.333 --> 00:44:11.090
with the cubes neatly separated into different sizes.

00:44:11.820 --> 00:44:15.740
Many of LeWitt's notebook pages depict earlier versions of this inventory.

00:44:16.140 --> 00:44:19.156
Early attempts have a loose, semi disorganized feeling,

00:44:19.156 --> 00:44:22.940
while later versions become more regimented and carefully annotated.

00:44:23.180 --> 00:44:27.020
Together they provide us with a high fidelity record of his discovery process.

00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:30.204
We can see one interesting development by comparing the

00:44:30.204 --> 00:44:32.780
three part cubes in an earlier and a later inventory.

00:44:33.020 --> 00:44:35.197
Notice in the second case that LeWitt seems to have

00:44:35.197 --> 00:44:37.630
found a new three part cube that he'd overlooked before.

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:38.100
.

00:44:38.420 --> 00:44:39.540
Where did this come from?

00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:42.820
Consider this squiggly three part open cube.

00:44:42.900 --> 00:44:46.100
Let's hold a mirror up to it and observe its mirror image.

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:49.554
Clearly, these shapes look similar, but what LeWitt discovered

00:44:49.554 --> 00:44:52.820
at some point is that they cannot be rotated into one another.

00:44:53.539 --> 00:44:55.220
Every shape has a mirror image.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:59.220
And apparently some mirror image pairs are not rotationally equivalent.

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:01.770
On the other hand, notice that the other three part

00:45:01.770 --> 00:45:04.240
incomplete cube is equivalent to its mirror image.

00:45:04.790 --> 00:45:07.190
In general, there doesn't seem to be a fixed rule here.

00:45:07.350 --> 00:45:09.190
For any incomplete open cube,

00:45:09.190 --> 00:45:10.950
we can consider its mirror image.

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:13.990
Sometimes the mirror image will be rotationally equivalent.

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:17.350
Other times it turns out to be a genuinely distinct shape.

00:45:17.990 --> 00:45:21.410
This realization was an epiphany for LeWitt, meaning a sudden

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:25.110
unplanned flash of insight that instantly transforms the problem.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:29.670
I want to suggest that this is probably not the kind of thing you realize gradually.

00:45:29.920 --> 00:45:32.503
Rather, I suspect there was a very specific moment when

00:45:32.503 --> 00:45:35.040
LeWitt realized that mirror images could be distinct.

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:38.960
And this event is marked by a sudden change in the available documentation.

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:44.269
Basically overnight, his tables undergo a dramatic growth spurt as he discovers that

00:45:44.269 --> 00:45:49.040
many of the cubes he'd found had undiscovered rotationally distinct mirror images.

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:54.880
Sometime in early 1974, Lewitt's enumeration had reached its final form.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:46:00.010
From there, we could say he formalized his result by creating a number of artworks.

00:46:00.330 --> 00:46:03.331
We have already seen the table of incomplete open cubes,

00:46:03.331 --> 00:46:06.010
and this existed in at least two different forms.

00:46:06.170 --> 00:46:09.770
But LeWitt also celebrated the result with a number of other creations.

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:14.570
He produced 40 inch painted aluminum sculptures of each of the variations.

00:46:14.890 --> 00:46:17.450
He also created a series of isometric drawings.

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:22.791
These were collected along with black and white photographs into an art book shown here,

00:46:22.791 --> 00:46:27.003
which the critic Nicholas Baum described as a cross between a construction

00:46:27.003 --> 00:46:31.158
manual and a Rosetta stone. LeWitt's most abstract representation for the

00:46:31.158 --> 00:46:34.920
cubes compresses the isometric view down to a flat hexagonal form.

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:40.026
His table of these simplified logos gets rid of any sense of three dimensionality,

00:46:40.026 --> 00:46:43.240
leaving just the raw data of the incomplete open cubes.

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:47.562
One thing I can say, having just created a digital version of this work,

00:46:47.562 --> 00:46:52.056
is that this minimalist table was actually super convenient for entering the relevant

00:46:52.056 --> 00:46:52.370
data.

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:56.654
I came up with a numbering system for the lines of the hexagon and then

00:46:56.654 --> 00:47:00.450
just directly transcribed this table into code. A few clicks later,

00:47:00.450 --> 00:47:02.930
and there it is, my very own Sol LeWitt.

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:07.451
It would have been very mentally taxing to have to think about three dimensional

00:47:07.451 --> 00:47:11.291
cubes while entering this data, but was totally effortless to just read off

00:47:11.291 --> 00:47:15.130
this concise abstract information. For the purposes of working computation,

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:18.655
with his idea, the artist really did format his findings

00:47:18.655 --> 00:47:21.520
in the most useful abstract form I could have asked for.

00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:26.240
LeWitt himself ended up making practical use of this minimalist representation.

00:47:26.560 --> 00:47:29.693
This sheet may appear to be another problem solving effort,

00:47:29.693 --> 00:47:32.560
but notice the list of names down the left hand side.

00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:36.400
These are the names of fabricators for the large scale sculptures.

00:47:36.560 --> 00:47:39.840
This sheet was how LeWitt kept track of who was making what.

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:44.130
His simplified logograms made for a perfect, perfect, lightweight accounting system.

00:47:44.530 --> 00:47:47.730
Our mathematical journey hinged on two important observations.

00:47:48.050 --> 00:47:51.650
First, we noticed that the number of families is related to the number of lookalikes.

00:47:51.650 --> 00:47:54.448
Second, we had an epiphany that these lookalikes could

00:47:54.448 --> 00:47:57.090
be formulaically counted with a re-indexing trick.

00:47:57.410 --> 00:48:00.485
This turns out to be an example of a much more general counting

00:48:00.485 --> 00:48:03.170
technique from group theory known as Burnside's Lemma.

00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:07.730
This same logic can be applied in many situations beyond incomplete open cubes.

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:10.470
Consider an incomplete open tetrahedron.

00:48:10.710 --> 00:48:14.299
Following the same steps of listing transforms and counting lookalikes,

00:48:14.299 --> 00:48:18.343
we can quickly calculate that there are 12 rotationally unique families of such

00:48:18.343 --> 00:48:18.950
tetrahedra.

00:48:19.190 --> 00:48:24.809
If instead of on or off, we choose to color the edges of the cube red, yellow or blue,

00:48:24.809 --> 00:48:30.231
Burnside's Lemma tells us that we should expect to find 22,815 rotationally unique

00:48:30.231 --> 00:48:30.950
colorings.

00:48:31.190 --> 00:48:33.954
In more complex cases, the technique can be used to count

00:48:33.954 --> 00:48:36.670
up unique positional possibilities of the Rubik's Cube.

00:48:37.060 --> 00:48:40.295
Burnside's Lemma is a general purpose counting technique you

00:48:40.295 --> 00:48:43.477
can use anytime you find yourself decorating a symmetrical

00:48:43.477 --> 00:48:46.820
object and wanting to know exactly how many options you have.

00:48:47.220 --> 00:48:51.193
From a mathematical point of view, formalizing our result really means putting

00:48:51.193 --> 00:48:55.676
together correct proofs for all of our assertions, our solving process was often messy,

00:48:55.676 --> 00:48:59.700
with a lot of hunches and loose ends that we'd now have to go back and verify.

00:49:00.020 --> 00:49:03.940
For example, it seemed like there were 24 distinct orientations of a cube.

00:49:04.120 --> 00:49:05.800
But who's to say we didn't miss a few?

00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:08.040
We need to prove that this is the right number.

00:49:08.360 --> 00:49:10.679
Or we noticed that there tended to be a complete

00:49:10.679 --> 00:49:13.240
number of copies of a family in the family portrait.

00:49:13.240 --> 00:49:15.960
But how can we be sure there aren't exceptions to this pattern?

00:49:16.520 --> 00:49:20.199
A formally correct proof would contain proper proofs of each of these results.

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:23.281
The first is that the order of the cube group is 24,

00:49:23.281 --> 00:49:26.680
and the second is what's known as the orbit stabilizer theorem.

00:49:27.160 --> 00:49:31.212
Capturing our results in rigorous proofs is what makes our findings useful to others,

00:49:31.212 --> 00:49:35.311
but can contribute to the sense that the mathematician's work is formal and perhaps a

00:49:35.311 --> 00:49:35.740
bit dry.

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:39.874
By taking the time to see how a mathematical exploration actually unfolds,

00:49:39.874 --> 00:49:44.049
we've gotten to see a process that can have much in common with an artistic one,

00:49:44.049 --> 00:49:48.378
with its guesswork and experiments, emotional ups and downs and moments of creative

00:49:48.378 --> 00:49:49.100
breakthrough.

00:49:55.180 --> 00:50:00.220
In the end, we found that there must be 218 families of rotationally equivalent cubes.

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:04.000
What happens to this number if we bring back the other constraints?

00:50:04.240 --> 00:50:07.986
A 2014 paper from Kansas State University applies an exhaustive

00:50:07.986 --> 00:50:12.327
computational search and confirms that with all the constraints applied,

00:50:12.327 --> 00:50:15.360
Sol LeWitt's finding of 122 is the correct number.

00:50:16.080 --> 00:50:18.585
But interestingly, although that number is correct,

00:50:18.585 --> 00:50:21.680
it turns out there's actually an error in the final sculpture.

00:50:21.840 --> 00:50:27.600
The cubes labeled 104 and 105 are actually the same incomplete open cube, a duplicate.

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:30.240
Meanwhile, this cube is nowhere to be found.

00:50:31.360 --> 00:50:33.341
When I first read this, I remember feeling like

00:50:33.341 --> 00:50:35.280
there was something kind of perfect about it.

00:50:35.360 --> 00:50:38.610
Of course, it would have been a bummer if LeWitt had made a lot of mistakes,

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:42.160
but it also might have felt a little sterile if he'd just gotten it exactly right.

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:46.880
There's something elegant about this little grace note of imperfection right at the end.

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:49.680
So is this what mathematicians are for?

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:53.600
To give a pass fail grade to the artwork and point out all of the mistakes?

00:50:54.290 --> 00:50:57.725
Certainly this is one role the mathematician should be capable of playing,

00:50:57.725 --> 00:51:00.929
but with regard to this specific sculpture, I think the mathematical

00:51:00.929 --> 00:51:03.250
point of view has something richer to contribute.

00:51:03.890 --> 00:51:08.622
LeWitt's biographer, Larry Bloom, wrote that LeWitt's primary impact on contemporary art

00:51:08.622 --> 00:51:13.247
was the idea that the product of the mind is more significant than the product of the

00:51:13.247 --> 00:51:13.570
hand.

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:15.890
This is a work of conceptual art.

00:51:15.970 --> 00:51:18.581
The mathematical point of view is specifically a

00:51:18.581 --> 00:51:21.300
way of engaging with the art in conceptual terms.

00:51:21.620 --> 00:51:24.882
It contributes to the space of ideas around this piece and I

00:51:24.882 --> 00:51:28.580
believe adds something aesthetic to how we can appreciate the work.

00:51:28.740 --> 00:51:33.546
In LeWitt's own words, all intervening steps, scribbles, sketches, drawings,

00:51:33.546 --> 00:51:38.100
failed works, models, studies, thoughts, conversations are of interest.

00:51:38.660 --> 00:51:41.228
Those that show the thought process of the artist are

00:51:41.228 --> 00:51:43.700
sometimes more interesting than the final product.

00:51:45.790 --> 00:52:11.200
...

